# mangohud — built FROM SOURCE via a novadeck-owned PKGBUILD (packages/mangohud/PKGBUILD), NOT
# fetched from the holo monorepo: the curated holo aarch64 repos ship NO mangohud/mangoapp at all
# (verified against the pinned base's synced core/extra dbs — gamescope/mesa/openal resolve, `mango*`
# does not). So there is nothing to `pacman -S`; we build it ourselves from a checked-in recipe. See
# packages/README.md for the overlay mechanism and the holo-pacman-no-gtk2 note (don't assume a
# package resolves).
#
# Why ship it at all: it is the Deck-style FPS/perf overlay, driven by gamescope's `--mangoapp` (the
# session passes it; SteamUI's Performance settings toggle the level over the GAMESCOPE_STATS pipe).
# It is NOT cosmetic on this device: the Steam client prepends `mangohud` to every game launch
# command when the Quick-Access performance overlay is enabled, so with no `mangohud` on the image
# toggling the overlay makes the exec fail and CRASHES the launch (HW 2026-07-07, Gravity Circuit via
# the Proton compat tool — ran fine with the overlay off, died the moment it was toggled on). See TODO.md and
# [[sm8650-gamescope-session-plumbing]].
#
# Recipe: MangoHud 0.8.4 (the exact version Fedora + Arch ship), built with
# -Dmangoapp=true -Dmangohudctl=true and system spdlog. The x86/NVIDIA bits are turned off
# (-Dwith_xnvctrl=disabled) since this is an arm64 Adreno device, and mangoplot is disabled
# (-Dmangoplot=disabled): it is a desktop PNG-graph helper whose matplotlib+numpy+BLAS/LAPACK chain
# bloats the image ~120MB for zero handheld value — the overlay (mangoapp) links none of it. All
# build/runtime deps resolve in the holo `core`/`extra` repos (dbus fmt glfw spdlog vulkan-* libx11
# libxkbcommon wayland appstream glslang libxrandr nlohmann-json python-mako ...).
#
# The 6 patches teach MangoHud to read a Qualcomm SoC's stats — WITHOUT them the overlay shows no /
# garbage GPU/battery/RAM data on an Adreno part. They are an upstream Qualcomm/SM85xx patch set
# (see packages/mangohud/patches/README.md for what each one does). We take ALL
# of them, including the SM8750 battery patch: SM8650 (Adreno 750) shares the Adreno devfreq/kgsl +
# hwmon layout those patches target, so they are the closest match and the right starting point —
# tune the exact sysfs paths on HW if a field reads wrong (see the patches README). `0002` is no
# longer SM8550-specific: it now probes a LIST of kgsl/devfreq nodes and takes the first that
# exists, which covers SM8650 by name rather than by "close enough to SM8550".
#
# Fields (see packages/README.md and packages/gamescope/source.pin):
#   name           pkgname (must match the PKGBUILD); build-dir + repo entry
#   pkgbuild_local path (relative to this dir) to the checked-in PKGBUILD to build
#   pkgrel_suffix  numeric suffix appended to pkgrel so pacman prefers our build
#   patches        space-separated files under ./patches/, applied `patch -p1` in the MangoHud
#                  source root (after `cd MangoHud` in prepare()), in the order listed.
#
# To bump mangohud: edit pkgver in packages/mangohud/PKGBUILD, re-verify the 6 patches apply against
# the new tag (patch -p1 --dry-run on a shallow clone), and re-roll any patch whose hunk fails.
name: mangohud
pkgbuild_local: PKGBUILD
pkgrel_suffix: 1
patches: 0001-Qualcomm-GPU-support.patch 0002-GPU-monitoring.patch 0003-Battery-name.patch 0004-Qualcomm-battery-power-now.patch 0005-RAM-name.patch 0006-SM8750-Battery.patch
